  "parent": {
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What your child is learning",
        "text": "To multiply by 4 by doubling a 2s fact. Because 4 = 2 + 2, a 4s fact is two equal 2s facts: 4 × 7 = 2 × 7 + 2 × 7 = 14 + 14 = 28. When the 4 comes second (6 × 4), break the 4 the same way: 6 × 2 + 6 × 2."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Try it with real things",
        "list": [
          "Make 2 rows of 7 pennies. Count them (14). Make 2 more rows below. Now there are 4 rows: 14 + 14 = 28.",
          "Practice doubles out loud: double 12, double 16, double 18."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Questions to ask",
        "list": [
          "Where is the 4?",
          "What is the 2s fact?",
          "What is double that number?"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Common mistakes",
        "list": [
          "Stopping at the 2s fact (2 × 7 = 14) and not doubling.",
          "Adding the factors: 4 + 7 = 11.",
          "Writing the second part as a number instead of a fact: 2 × 7 + 2.",
          "Doubling the wrong fact, such as 4 × 2 for 4 × 9."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "A note on notation",
        "text": "Some books write the facts without ( ) and some with ( ). Both mean the same thing. Study Scoop uses original practice questions; it never copies the textbook."
      }
    ]
  }
